--- Quick Revision Notes ---
OOP stands for Object-Oriented Programming.
It helps organize code by grouping related data and behavior into objects.

Main ideas:
- Class: a blueprint for creating objects
- Object: an instance of a class
- Attribute: data stored in an object
- Method: a function inside a class

Example:
class Dog:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

    def bark(self):
        print("Woof")

Important concepts:
- __init__() runs automatically when an object is created
- self refers to the current object
- instance variables belong to each object
- class variables belong to the whole class

Other concepts:
- Encapsulation: keep data and methods together
- Inheritance: one class can use features of another class
- Polymorphism: different classes can use the same method name differently
- __str__() is used to define a readable string representation of an object

Remember:
OOP makes code easier to reuse, manage, and understand.


